\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The discrete wedge and the cup product}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{The primal wedge of
\citet{Lemma 8.1, p. 17}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}
satisfies #{\alpha\wedge\beta=(-1)^{kl}\beta\wedge\alpha},
where #{k} and #{l} are the degrees. It obeys
#{\extd(\alpha\wedge\beta)=(\extd\alpha)\wedge\beta+(-1)^{k}\alpha\wedge(\extd\beta)},
\citet{Lemma 8.2, p. 19}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec},
and it is associative for closed forms,
\citet{Lemma 8.3, p. 20}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}.
For a mesh of characteristic length #{\Delta x}, the average
associativity defect is of order #{\Delta x},
\citet{Remark 8.1, p. 21}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}.
That bound is stated for discretizations of constant forms,
which are closed. It is not a bound for an arbitrary
cochain.}

\p{The cup product of \ref{fdip-0093} is the simplicial
front-and-back product over a commutative ring. It is not
graded-commutative on cochains. It is associative: both
triple products read #{\alpha} on #{v_0,\ldots,v_k},
#{\beta} on #{v_k,\ldots,v_{k+l}} and #{\gamma} on
#{v_{k+l},\ldots,v_{k+l+m}}.}

\p{Scott O. Wilson works with real simplicial cochains of a smooth
triangulation of a Riemannian manifold whose fullness is bounded
below by a positive constant
\citet{Definition 4.3, p. 6}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
His constants depend on that bound. His product is the
cochain whose value on a simplex is the integral of the wedge
of their Whitney forms
\citet{Definition 5.1, p. 8}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
It is graded commutative, his coboundary is a derivation of it,
and on cohomology it agrees with the Alexander–Whitney product.
On an arbitrary cochain it is not associative: one association
in his example vanishes and the other equals #{-1/4} times the
remaining cochain
\citet{Example 5.8, p. 14}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
The combinatorial coefficient is the product of the factorials
of the two degrees, divided by the factorial of one more than
their sum
\citet{Theorem 5.2, p. 9}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
Those coefficients are rational, so the formula is not stated
over every commutative ring. On each top-dimensional simplex,
the wedge of two smooth forms is approximated to order the mesh
\citet{Theorem 5.4, p. 10}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
The integrated comparison and the failure of associativity, for
cochains obtained by integrating smooth forms, are of the same
order in the mesh
\citet{Corollary 5.7 and Theorem 5.9, p. 14}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
Those bounds are not claimed for an arbitrary cochain. The cup
product of \ref{fdip-0093} is the associative product of the
preceding paragraph.}
